# vault-credentials.example — the SECRETS agent-vault brokers on this stack's behalf. One file per
# stack, beside `mounts` and `service-env`, and the only place the real values are written down.
#
#     cp vault-credentials.example vault-credentials
#     chmod 0600 vault-credentials
#     $EDITOR vault-credentials
#     ./vault-sync.sh                 # push them into the running agent-vault
#
# It is an EXAMPLE, like mounts.example / service-env.example: the real file holds credentials, so it
# is never committed (see .gitignore). Under Ansible it is templated from the project's repo with the
# values pulled from the vault, at mode 0600.
#
# WHAT MAKES THIS FILE DIFFERENT FROM service-env. service-env is handed to the hub and to every box
# — anything in it is readable by an agent, which is the whole point for a database URL and the whole
# problem for an API token. The values HERE never enter a container an agent can read. They are
# pushed into agent-vault by ./vault-sync.sh and injected into outbound requests at the proxy
# boundary; the box only ever holds a placeholder like __grafana_sa_token__.
#
# Format is the same as service-env — compose's env_file format, so the two files read alike:
#   * one KEY=VALUE per line; the value is LITERAL to the end of the line
#   * '#' comments and blank lines ignored
#   * no quoting rules, no shell/${} expansion, and values may not contain newlines
#   * keys must match [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*  (a bad key is a hard error, not a silent skip)
#
# The KEY is what `vault-services` references — an auth block names a credential key, never a value.
# Keep the two files in step: agent-vault rejects a service that references a key it cannot resolve,
# so vault-sync.sh pushes credentials FIRST and services second.
#
# NOTHING READS THIS FILE BUT vault-sync.sh, and only when you run it. agent-vault keeps its own
# encrypted store and answers from that, so once a sync has succeeded this copy is a duplicate that
# reads like the source of the values and is not — edit it, forget the sync, and the vault serves the
# old one forever. A stack that GENERATES this file (Ansible templating it from a secret store) should
# therefore hand vault-sync.sh `--shred-credentials`, which deletes it after a successful push; the
# generator is the source of truth and the server's copy is an artifact. Leave the flag off here,
# where the file is written by hand and is the only copy there is.
#
# Removing a line here does NOT delete the credential from the vault; `vault-sync.sh` only sets what
# it is given, so a key that once existed keeps its old value until you delete it explicitly:
#     docker compose exec agent-vault agent-vault vault credential delete OLD_KEY --vault <vault>
# That asymmetry is deliberate — a templating slip that renders this file empty must not silently
# strip a live stack of every credential it brokers.

# ---- example: a Grafana service account (bearer token on the API) ----
#GRAFANA_SA_TOKEN=glsa_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# ---- example: HTTP basic auth for the same Grafana's login page ----
# A viewer-only account. The broker base64s the pair into an Authorization header; neither half ever
# reaches the agent.
#GRAFANA_HTTP_USER=viewer
#GRAFANA_HTTP_PASS=xxxxxxxx

# ---- example: an OAuth client secret posted in a token-exchange body ----
# The client ID is not a secret and can stay in service-env; the secret belongs here, and
# vault-services declares a `body` substitution so the broker swaps it into the POST form.
#ASANA_CLIENT_SECRET=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
